{{also|Burg|-burg}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-burg.ogg|a=AU}}

===Etymology 1===
The historical sense is from {{der|en|LL.|burgus}}, from {{der|en|frk|*burg}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*burgz||borough, fortification}}. {{doublet|en|borough|Brough|burgh|burh|bury}}. Also compare {{m|en|burgess}}.

The modern sense may have been formed in part by analogy with the many North American city names that are suffixed with {{m|en|-burg}} (a number of which in the {{w|Eastern United States}} once used ''-burgh'' instead. See {{m|en|burgh}}), as well as being formed in part due to {{noncog|de|Burg}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|North America}} A [[city]] or [[town]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1921| year_published=2012| edition=HTML| author=w:Edgar Rice Burroughs| title=The Efficiency Expert| url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3475/3475-h/3475-h.htm| publisher=The Gutenberg Project| passage=Tell mother that I will write her in a day or two, probably from Chicago, as I have always had an idea that that was one '''burg''' where I could make good.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2009|month=June|author=David Thriault| title=This Way In: The Sound and the Fury| volume=151| issue=6| page=6| magazine=w:Esquire| passage=Imagine my surprise when I learned that he was not only a Canadian but lived in Ottawa, that icy '''burg''' I had left so many kilometers -- sorry, miles -- behind me.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2010|month=Feb|author=Paige Orloff| title=Big Style on a (Little) Budget| volume=33| issue=2 |page=84| magazine=Country Living| passage=It's been said that Wilder modeled that fictional setting on Peterborough, a quaint '''burg''' tucked away in New Hampshire's verdant southwestern hills.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} A [[fortified]] town in [[medieval]] Europe.

